{:edb/db-before-t 2593 :edb/db-after-t 2594 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535906 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:24:04.390Z" 13194139535906 true] [17592186157026 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14037357/assets/96cd980d105fe04d2169f9252aa2e7e08bf57ba18cf7d7808d0c4c98c83f7767.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{ 1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $Y \\setminus X.$" 13194139535906 false] [17592186157026 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071857/assets/96cd980d105fe04d2169f9252aa2e7e08bf57ba18cf7d7808d0c4c98c83f7767.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{ 1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $Y \\setminus X.$" 13194139535906 true] [17592186157026 1145 "The difference $Y \\setminus X$ consists of all the elements that are in $Y$ but not $X.$ To represent $Y \\setminus X,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in $Y$ but not $X.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14037357/assets/5461edca3823f1cafd9f7b51528cd6eb1035e38f7f94c60177e369e2cdd5f350.png)\n\nThe only element in $Y$ that is not in $X$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nY \\setminus X = \\{ 4 \\}.\n$$" 13194139535906 false] [17592186157026 1145 "The difference $Y \\setminus X$ consists of all the elements that are in $Y$ but not $X.$ To represent $Y \\setminus X,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in $Y$ but not $X.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071857/assets/5461edca3823f1cafd9f7b51528cd6eb1035e38f7f94c60177e369e2cdd5f350.png)\n\nThe only element in $Y$ that is not in $X$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nY \\setminus X = \\{ 4 \\}.\n$$" 13194139535906 true] [17592186157218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373754 13194139535906 true] [17592186157218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373768 13194139535906 true] [17592186157218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373775 13194139535906 true] [17592186157218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373782 13194139535906 true] [17592186157218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373789 13194139535906 true] [17592186157219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373761 13194139535906 true] [17592186157219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373796 13194139535906 true] [17592186157219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373831 13194139535906 true] [17592186157223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373803 13194139535906 true] [17592186157223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373810 13194139535906 true] [17592186157223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373817 13194139535906 true] [17592186157223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373824 13194139535906 true] [17592186157706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535906 true] [17592186157706 1100 "Let $A$ be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $B$ be the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0$. What is $B∖A$?" 13194139535906 false] [17592186157706 1100 "Let $A$ be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $B$ be the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0.$ What is $B∖A?$" 13194139535906 true] [17592186157706 1145 "The multiples of $10$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA={10,20,30,40,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $B∖A$ is the *difference* of $B$ and $A,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $B$ but *not* in $A.$\n\nThe elements of $B$ that are also in $A$ are $10,20,30,40,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $10$). So, we must remove these elements from $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∖A & =B-A \\\\  & ={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,…}-{10,20,30,40,…} \\\\  & ={5,15,25,35,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535906 true] [17592186296422 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14037357/assets/a50acd7e54d52f6284962cb81a5f662321b045a5d533470186719ad2a91ab18b.png)\n\nGiven the sets $Y={△,◻,♣,♡}$ and  $Z={△,◻,♡,♢,♠}$ shown above, 